/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:923:%In this section we analyze the runtime complexity of Algorithm~\ref{algo:main}.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:927:The runtime of MULE (Algorithm~\ref{algo:main}) on an input graph of $n$ vertices is $O\left (n \cdot 2^n \right)$.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:931:MULE initializes variables and calls to Algorithm~\ref{algo:dfs}, hence we analyze the runtime of Algorithm~\ref{algo:dfs}. An execution of the recursive Algorithm~\ref{algo:dfs} can be viewed as a search tree as follows. Each call to Enum-Uncertain-MC is a node of this search tree. The first call to the method is the root node. A node in this search tree is either an internal node that makes one or more recursive calls, or a leaf node that does not make further recursive calls. To analyze the runtime of Algorithm~\ref{algo:dfs}, we consider the time spent at internal nodes as well as leaf nodes.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:933:The runtime at each leaf node is $O(1)$. For a leaf node, the parameter $I = \emptyset$, and there are no further recursive calls. This implies that either $C$ is $\alpha$-maximal ($X = \emptyset$) and is emitted in line~\ref{algoline:out} or it is non-maximal ($X \neq \emptyset$) but cannot be extended by the loop in line~\ref{algoline:loop} as $I = \emptyset$. Checking the sizes of $I$ and $X$ takes constant time.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:935:We next consider the time taken at each internal node. Instead of adding up the times at different internal nodes, we equivalently add up the cost of the different edges in the search tree. At each internal node, the cost of making a recursive call can be analyzed as follows. Line~\ref{algoline:genc} takes $O \left ( n \right )$ time as we add all vertices in $C$ to $C'$ and also $u$. Line~\ref{algoline:prob} takes constant time. Lines~\ref{algoline:genI}~and~\ref{algoline:genX} take $O \left ( n \right )$ time (Lemmas~\ref{lemma:complexity1}~and~\ref{lemma:complexity2} respectively). Note that lines~\ref{algoline:genc}~to~\ref{algoline:genX} can get executed only once in between the two calls. Thus total runtime for each edge of the search tree is $O\left ( n \right )$.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:937:Note that the total number of calls made to the method method Enum-Uncertain-MC is no more than the possible number of unique subsets of $V$, which is  $O \left ( 2^n \right )$. We see that for internal nodes, time complexity is $O \left ( n \right )$ and for leaf nodes it is $O \left ( 1 \right )$. Hence the time complexity of Algorithm~\ref{algo:dfs} is $O \left ( n \cdot 2^n \right )$. 
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:942:Algorithms~\ref{algo:generateI}~and~\ref{algo:generateX} is $O \left ( n \right )$.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:943:We prove the same in Lemmas~\ref{lemma:complexity1}~and~\ref{lemma:complexity2}
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:948:The runtime of Algorithm~\ref{algo:generateI} is $O \left ( n \right )$.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:954:and hence the loop at line 4 of Algorithm~\ref{algo:generateI}
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:959:Assume that it takes constant time to find out the probability of an edge.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:969:The runtime of Algorithm~\ref{algo:generateX} is $O \left ( n \right )$.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:973:of Lemma~\ref{lemma:complexity1}.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:977:The worst-case runtime of any algorithm that can output all maximal cliques of an uncertain graph on $n$ vertices is $\Omega \left ( \sqrt{n} \cdot 2^n \right )$.
