/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1005:For a typical input graph, many maximal cliques are small, and may not be interesting to the user. Hence it is helpful to have an algorithm that can enumerate only large maximal cliques efficiently, rather than enumerate all maximal cliques. We now describe an algorithm that enumerates every $\alpha$-maximal clique with more than $t$ vertices, where $t$ is an user provided parameter.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1010:Algorithm~\ref{algo:largemule} runs on the pruned uncertain graph $\ugraph'$ to enumerate only large maximal cliques. The recursive method in Algorithm~\ref{algo:large} differs from Algorithm~\ref{algo:dfs} as follows. Before each recursive call to method Enum-Uncertain-MC-Large (Algorithm~\ref{algo:large}), the algorithm checks if the sum of the sizes of the current working clique $C'$ and the candidate vertex set $I'$ are greater than the size threshold $t$. If not, the recursive method is not called. This optimization leads to a substantial pruning of the search space and hence a reduction in runtime.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1013:\begin{algorithm}[ht]
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1014:\caption{LARGE--MULE($\ugraph,\alpha$,$t$)}
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1016:\KwIn{$\alpha, 0 < \alpha < 1$ is the user provided probability threshold}
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1028:\end{algorithm}
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1032:\begin{algorithm}[ht]
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1035:\KwIn{$q \mbox{ is pre-computed } \cliqueprob(C,\ugraph)$}
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1036:\KwIn{$I \mbox{ is a set of tuples} \left(u,r\right)$, such that $\forall (u,r) \in I$, $u > \max(C)$, and $\cliqueprob(C \cup \{ u\},\ugraph) = q \cdot r \ge \alpha$, i.e. $C \cup \{ u \}$ is an $\alpha$-clique in $\ugraph$}
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1037:\KwIn{$X \mbox{ is a set of tuples} \left(v,s\right)$, such that $\forall (v,s) \in X$, $v \not \in C$, $v < \max(C)$, and $\cliqueprob(C \cup \{v\},\ugraph) = q \cdot s \ge \alpha$ , i.e. $C \cup \{ v \}$ is an $\alpha$-clique in $\ugraph$}
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1043:            Output $C$ as $\alpha$-maximal clique  \;
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1050:                $q' \leftarrow q \cdot r$  \tcp{$\cliqueprob(C \cup \{ v \},\ugraph)$}  
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1062:\end{algorithm}
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1068:Given an input graph $\ugraph$, LARGE--MULE (Algorithm~\ref{algo:largemule}) enumerates every $\alpha$-maximal clique with more than $t$ vertices.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1074:First we prove that no maximal clique of size less than $t$ is enumerated by Algorithm~\ref{algo:large}. Consider an $\alpha$-maximal clique $C_1$ in $\ugraph$ with less than $t$ vertices. Also let $m_1 = \max(C_1)$ and $C_1' = C_1 \setminus \{ m_1 \}$.  Note that if $C_1$ is emitted by Algorithm~\ref{algo:large}, then a call must be made to Enum-Uncertain-MC-Large with $C_1$. Since the Algorithm adds vertices in lexicographical ordering, this implies that a call must be made to Enum-Uncertain-MC-Large with $C_1'$ before the call is made with $C_1$. In the worst case, let us consider that the search tree reaches the execution point where Enum-Uncertain-MC-Large is called with $C_1'$. Consider the execution of the algorithm where $m_1$ is added to $C = C_1'$ to form $C' = C_1$. Since $C_1$ is an $\alpha$-maximal clique, $I'$ will become NULL which implies $\left | I' \right | = 0$. We know that $\left | C_1 \right | < t$. Thus $\left | C_1 + I' \right |$ will also be less than $t$ and the If condition (line 8) will succeed. This will result in the execution of the continue statement. Thus Enum-Uncertain-MC-Large will not be called with $C_1$ implying that $C_1$ is not enumerated.
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1076:Next we show that any maximal clique of size at least $t$ is enumerated by Algorithm~\ref{algo:large}. Consider an $\alpha$-maximal clique $C_2$ in $\ugraph$ of size at least $t$. We note that the ``If'' condition in line 8 is never satisfied in the search path ending with $C_2$ and hence a call is made to the method with Enum-Uncertain-MC-Large with $C_2$. This is easy to see as whenever a call is made to Enum-Uncertain-MC-Large with any $C \subseteq C_2$, since $C_2$ is large, we always have $\left | C \right | + \left | I \right | \ge t$. 
/tmp/tmp.KDAB96EtOn/src/maximal-clique.tex:1085:We report the results of an experimental evaluation of our algorithm. We implemented the algorithm using Java. We ran all experiments on a system with a 3.19 GHz Intel(R) Core(TM) i5 processor and 4 GB of RAM, with heap space configured at 1.5GB.
